20:26:00 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: Great question! There are actually two different things going on—and a common misconception about cabin pressure that explains the ear popping. Let me break it down. ### Why the cabin air is so dry At 35,000 feet, the outside air is extremely cold (around -50°F/-45°C) and holds almost no moisture—its absolute humidity is near zero. The cabin gets fresh air from the engines (called bleed air), which comes from that same outside air. When this ultra-dry, cold air is compressed and warmed to a comfortable cabin temperature, its **relative humidity** plummets—typically to around 10–20%, which is drier than most deserts. Even the recirculated air in the cabin goes through filters, but no significant moisture is added back. The result: your skin, eyes, and throat feel parched. ### Why your ears pop (even though the cabin is pressurized) Here’s the key: **cabin pressure does not stay at sea-level pressure.** It’s maintained at a pressure equivalent to being at an altitude of about 6,000–8,000 feet (the “cabin altitude”). So while the plane climbs to 35,000 ft, the cabin pressure is *decreased* to that lower altitude equivalent. When the plane descends, the cabin pressure is *increased* back toward ground level. Your ear popping is your Eustachian tubes opening to equalize the pressure on either side of your eardrum. That happens whenever the cabin pressure changes significantly—on ascent (pressure drops, ears feel full) and on descent (pressure rises, ears need to pop again). It’s the same phenomenon you’d feel driving up a mountain, just happening faster. If the cabin pressure truly stayed completely constant at sea level, your ears wouldn’t pop—but that would put much more stress on the aircraft structure, so aircraft are designed to let the cabin altitude rise a bit for efficiency and safety. So, dry air comes from the moisture-starved outside air; ear popping comes from the changing cabin altitude during takeoff and landing, not from a constant pressure. Hope that clears it up!
