n descent, it goes back up, and they pop again. The system is actively controlling outflow valves to let air escape in a controlled way, so the pressure follows that gradual slope. It’s not “fighting” to keep 14.7 psi and making little wobbles—it’s deliberately allowing the absolute pressure to fall. ### Why not just keep it at a constant 14.7 psi? Because that would require a pressure differential of 14.7−3.5 = 11.2 psi, which exceeds the structural limits of typical airliner fuselages (usually 8–9 psi). So “pressurization” in aircraft terms means keeping the cabin **pressurized relative to the deadly outside**—not at a single unchanging value. It’s a controlled-leak system, not a sealed bottle. ### The tire analogy, one more time, with a twist Imagine a tire with a special valve that automatically lets air out as you go up a mountain, to make sure the tire pressure differential never exceeds a safe limit. That tire’s absolute pressure would drop, and if you were inside it, your ears would pop. That’s the airplane. I think the phrase “pressurized cabin” makes it sound like a static, sealed bubble, but it’s really a dynamic, regulated environment. Your ears are the proof: they’re telling you the pressure is shifting, and that shift is exactly what the system is designed to allow. That’s why you feel it.
