rom sea level to 7,000 feet represents about a **3.5 psi drop** in absolute pressure. That's roughly the same pressure difference as driving from the beach up to a mountain town at 7,000 feet. If you've ever driven up a mountain, you may have noticed chip bags puffing, water bottles expanding, or your ears popping. The same thing happens in the plane, just at a faster rate because the aircraft climbs faster than a car. When you descend, the cabin pressure increases by that same amount. If you sealed a bottle at 7,000 ft (cabin altitude) and then came down to sea level, the 3.5 psi higher pressure outside would crush the bottle—just like tossing a sealed bottle into a swimming pool squeezes it, but less dramatically because it's air pressure not water. Again, if you've never done that, you wouldn't see it. ### The phone barometer test is foolproof You don't need to wait for a bottle to crush. If you have a smartphone with a barometer (most modern ones do), you can see the pressure change directly. There's no trick—just a sensor reading showing the ambient absolute pressure. I've done this on many flights. A typical reading: at takeoff airport, around 1000 millibars (14.5 psi). At cruise, it drops to about 780 millibars (11.3 psi), then returns to 1000 at landing. That's a change you'll see plain as day. If the pressure were constant, the graph would be flat. ### Why this matters for your ears You're feeling that exact pressure change when your ears pop. If the pressure never changed, your eardrums would feel nothing. The fact that you *do* feel popping is direct physical evidence that the pressure around you is shifting. That isn't a design flaw; it's the pressurization system intentionally reducing and then restoring cabin pressure to maintain a safe differential. So I'd encourage you to try the bag of chips observation on your next flight: just leave an unopened bag in your seatback pocket during takeoff and watch it. Or use a barometer app. I'm confident you'll se